'''Prem Singh Yadav''' is an Indian scientist whose research focuses on [[Cloning|buffalo cloning]]<ref name="src-d03b0f5819">{{cite web|url=https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/india/cibr-scientists-clone-7-elite-m-29-buffaloes-re-clone-one-5561561.html|title=CIBR scientists clone 7 elite M-29 buffaloes, re-clone one|website=Moneycontrol|date=17 July 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220929222324/https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/india/cibr-scientists-clone-7-elite-m-29-buffaloes-re-clone-one-5561561.html|archive-date=29 September 2022}}</ref>.
Yadav headed the buffalo-cloning team at [[Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|ICAR-CIRB]] in [[Hissar|Hisar]]<ref name="src-d03b0f5819" />.
Yadav served as principal investigator of the Hisar Gaurav cloning project, whose semen yielded about 14,000 doses distributed free to Nuh farmers<ref name="src-5371d6d304">{{cite web|url=https://theprint.in/india/hisar-gaurav-first-cloned-calf-born-at-icar-cirb-turns-7/1260625/|title='Hisar Gaurav', first cloned calf born at ICAR-CIRB, turns 7|website=theprint.in|date=11 December 2022}}</ref>.
In 2023, the [[Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|CIRB]] cloning initiative led by Yadav was registered in the [[Limca Book of Records]] for producing the maximum number of cloned calves from a single breeding bull<ref name="src-dc3c3cfdc3">{{cite web|url=https://www.amarujala.com/photo-gallery/haryana/hisar/cirb-registered-in-limca-book-for-maximum-clone-for-one-bull-2023-03-15|title=CIRB registered in Limca Book for maximum clone for one bull|website=Amar Ujala|date=2023}}</ref>.

==Early life==
Yadav is from the village of Nimoth in Rewari district, Haryana<ref name="src-30cdb84a23">{{cite web|url=https://www.amarujala.com/haryana/rewari/principal-scientist-dr-prem-singh-retired-rewari-news-c-198-1-rew1001-218880-2025-05-03|title=प्रधान वैज्ञानिक डॉ. प्रेम सिंह हुए सेवानिवृत्त|website=Amar Ujala|date=3 May 2025}}</ref>.

==Career==
Yadav joined the Indian Council of Agricultural Research as a scientist on 12 April 1993<ref name="src-e73557644a">{{cite web|url=https://cirb.res.in/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/Staff-Remuneration-List-Hisar-2023.pdf|title=Scientists Remunerations|website=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|date=2023}}</ref>. In 2003, while a senior scientist, Yadav began a 12-month Department of Biotechnology overseas associateship in [[Neustadt, Hesse|Neustadt]], Germany<ref name="src-75cd616383">{{cite web|url=https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/inline-files/PARTNERSHIP-AND-LINKAGES-02_0.pdf|title=ICAR Partnership and Linkages: Overseas training during 2003–04|website=Indian Council of Agricultural Research|date=2003}}</ref>. Yadav undertook a research stay at the Institute for Farm Animal Genetics in Germany from 28 December 2010 to 10 February 2011<ref name="src-4fdcf94f36">{{cite web|url=https://cirb.res.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/annualreport2010-2011.pdf|title=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes Annual Report 2010–11|website=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|date=2010–11}}</ref>. Yadav served as a CIRB scientist leading scientific training in reproductive biotechnology and somatic cell nuclear transfer for ASEAN delegations<ref name="src-ee4633ec4f">{{cite web|url=https://cirb.res.in/wp-content/uploads/2018/01/AnnualReport2016-17.pdf|title=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes (CIRB) Annual Report 2016-17|website=ICAR - Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|date=2017}}</ref>. The Tribune identified Prem Singh Yadav as a principal scientist and project head at ICAR-CIRB in 2018<ref name="src-12f91675f5">{{cite web|url=https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/archive/haryana/cloned-bull-s-semen-used-to-produce-20-calves-in-8-months-698521/|title=Cloned bull's semen used to produce 20 calves in 8 months|website=The Tribune|date=14 December 2018}}</ref>. Yadav served as a Principal Scientist presenting CIRB-NDRI joint cloning results at the 2019 national brainstorming session on cloned-bull semen in New Delhi<ref name="src-faf366d880">{{cite web|url=https://epashupalan.com/2525/news/brainstorming-session-on-use-of-cloned-bull-semen-for-breed-improvement/|title=Brainstorming Session on Use of cloned Bull Semen for Breed Improvement|website=ePashupalan|date=30 August 2019}}</ref>. He later served as head of [[Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|ICAR-CIRB]]'s Division of Animal Physiology and Reproduction<ref name="src-87ac8ccc96">{{cite web|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210723192859/https://cirb.icar.gov.in/ps-yadav/|title=Dr. Prem Singh Yadav|website=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes (archived official profile)|date=23 July 2021}}</ref>. PTI described Prem Singh Yadav as principal investigator of the Hisar Gaurav cloning project in 2022<ref name="src-5371d6d304" />. Yadav remained principal investigator of the CIRB cloning project at Hisar Gaurav's 10th-birthday celebration in December 2024, by when 22,000 semen doses had been produced for artificial insemination<ref name="src-a8832a444b">{{cite web|url=https://www.bhaskar.com/local/haryana/hisar/news/cake-cut-on-hisar-gaurav-bull-birthday-in-haryana-134099151.html|title=bhaskar.com|website=bhaskar.com|date=10 December 2024}}</ref>. Yadav retired from [[Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|ICAR-CIRB]] as a principal scientist in 2025<ref name="src-30cdb84a23" />.

==Research==
Yadav led the CIRB programme that produced Hisar Gaurav on 11 December 2015, making CIRB India's second institute to clone a buffalo<ref name="src-72b5acbb17">{{cite web|url=https://www.tribuneindia.com/news/archive/haryana/cloned-murrah-bull-semen-successfully-used-for-ai-dr-prem-singh-yadav-589214|title=The Tribune India (Hisar Edition): Cloned Murrah Bull Hisar Gaurav Semen Successfully Used for AI under Dr. Prem Singh Yadav|website=The Tribune India (Hisar / Haryana)|date=13 March 2019}}</ref>. In 2015, a team led by Yadav produced Hisar Gaurav, a cloned [[Murrah buffalo|Murrah]] bull; ICAR described [[Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|CIRB]] as India's second centre to produce a cloned buffalo<ref name="src-443a3f8b23">{{cite web|url=https://icar.gov.in/node/4887|title=ICAR-CIRB become India’s second center to produce cloned buffalo|website=icar.gov.in|date=2016}}</ref>. ICAR-CIRB became the second research institute in India to clone a buffalo, following the National Dairy Research Institute<ref name="src-f5d713e1d9">{{cite web|url=https://www.dnaindia.com/india/report-cirb-becomes-india-s-second-centre-to-produce-a-cloned-buffalo-2164170|title=CIRB becomes India’s second centre to produce a cloned buffalo|website=Daily News and Analysis|date=10 January 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170106064115/http://www.dnaindia.com/india/report-cirb-becomes-india-s-second-centre-to-produce-a-cloned-buffalo-2164170|archive-date=6 January 2017}}</ref>. Sach-Gaurav was reported in 2018 as the first cloned buffalo calf produced in the field at an Indian dairy farm, with Yadav among the paper's co-authors<ref name="src-b4ab04664d">{{cite web|url=https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/indias-first-cloned-assamese-buffalo-born-1824454|title=India’s First Cloned Assamese Buffalo Born|website=NDTV|date=15 March 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190811220112/https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/indias-first-cloned-assamese-buffalo-born-1824454|archive-date=11 August 2019}}</ref>. Yadav led the CIRB team that cloned Hisar Gaurav, whose semen produced 20 calves through artificial insemination<ref name="src-89f4cef816">{{cite web|url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/city/gurgaon/clone-murrah-buffalo-hisar-gaurav-turns-three-has-produced-20-male-and-female-calves/articleshow/67084199.cms|title=1st clone male Murrah buffalo turns 3|website=The Times of India|date=14 December 2018}}</ref>. Yadav led the CIRB programme, as principal scientist and project head, in which Hisar Gaurav's semen produced 20 calves through artificial insemination in eight months<ref name="src-12f91675f5" />. Yadav led the CIRB cloning project whose Hisar-Gaurav semen tested fully viable on computerised parameters from 22 months of age<ref name="src-5c7724f723">{{cite web|url=https://hindi.krishijagran.com/news/vicky-donor-of-hissar-is-hissar-gaurav/|title=विक्की डोनर बना हिसार गौरव|website=Krishi Jagran Hindi|date=17 January 2018}}</ref>. Yadav led the CIRB cloning team that produced Sach-Gaurav, the first cloned Assamese buffalo born in the field<ref name="src-896640c10f">{{cite web|url=https://www.indiatimes.com/news/india/like-dolly-the-cloned-sheep-india-s-first-cloned-assamese-buffalo-is-born-341577.html|title=Like Dolly The Cloned Sheep, India's First Cloned Assamese Buffalo Is Born!|website=Indiatimes|date=16 March 2018}}</ref>. Yadav served as chief scientist of the CIRB cloning project that produced seven M-29 clones and a Hisar-Gaurav re-clone between October 2019 and January 2020<ref name="src-edb32eef83">{{cite web|url=https://www.amarujala.com/haryana/hisar/cirb-hisar-has-created-seven-cloned-pieces-one-re-clone-hisar-news-rtk5643533104|title=सीआईआरबी हिसार ने तैयार किए सात क्लोन कटड़े, एक री-क्लोन भी बनाया|website=Amar Ujala|date=17 July 2020}}</ref>. In 2020, a team led by Yadav produced seven clones of the elite breeding bull M-29 and a re-clone of Hisar Gaurav<ref name="src-d03b0f5819" />. Yadav led the CIRB cloning initiative that established India among the few countries with operational somatic cell nuclear transfer for buffalo<ref name="src-6ee08c1bb8">{{cite web|url=https://zeenews.india.com/india/cibr-hisar-achieves-a-milestone-clones-8-calves-to-improve-buffalo-breed-2296320.html|title=CIBR Hisar achieves a milestone, clones 8 calves to improve buffalo breeding|website=Zee News|date=2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200717125628/https://zeenews.india.com/india/cibr-hisar-achieves-a-milestone-clones-8-calves-to-improve-buffalo-breed-2296320.html|archive-date=17 July 2020}}</ref>. Yadav reported 10 cloned bulls, over 10,000 Hisar-Gaurav semen doses and 25 progenies produced under the NASF cloning project<ref name="src-edb32eef83" />. Yadav led the CIRB cloning programme to multiply proven buffalo bulls for genetic improvement of milk and meat production<ref name="src-1a851e9be8">{{cite web|url=https://www.business-standard.com/article/opinion/cloning-overcomes-prejudices-121072600018_1.html|title=Cloning overcomes prejudices|website=Business Standard|date=26 July 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210725203634/https://www.business-standard.com/article/opinion/cloning-overcomes-prejudices-121072600018_1.html|archive-date=25 July 2021}}</ref>. By 2021, ICAR-CIRB had produced ten cloned bulls under the cloning project headed by Yadav, aimed at improving breed and milk production<ref name="src-b96db52eb3">{{cite web|url=https://www.bhaskar.com/local/haryana/hisar/news/central-buffalo-research-institute-of-hisar-to-take-up-cowherds-of-up-punjab-and-rajasthan-to-increase-the-income-of-farmers-128124846.html|title=bhaskar.com|website=bhaskar.com|date=14 January 2021}}</ref>. By 2022, [[Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|CIRB]] had produced about 14,000 semen doses from Hisar Gaurav and distributed them free to farmers in villages of Nuh district<ref name="src-a26d426313">{{cite web|url=https://www.etvbharat.com/english/bharat/hisar-gaurav-first-cloned-calf-born-at-icar-cirb-turns-7/na20221211201520284284768|title='Hisar Gaurav', first cloned calf born at ICAR-CIRB, turns 7|website=ETV Bharat|date=11 December 2022}}</ref>. Hisar Gaurav was the first cloned buffalo calf produced at ICAR-CIRB under Yadav's biotechnology programme<ref name="src-a26d426313" />. Yadav contributed to the CIRB team that produced Veer Gaurav, a buffalo calf cloned from Hisar-Gaurav semen using ovum pick-up and in-vitro embryo production<ref name="src-ff49ea1698">{{cite web|url=https://icar.org.in/en/node/17212|title=ICAR-CIRB produces Veer Gaurav through OPU-IVEP technology|website=Indian Council of Agricultural Research|date=25 December 2022}}</ref>. Research led by Yadav found that female progeny of Hisar Gaurav produced 300–600 litres more milk per lactation<ref name="src-e4007d0265">{{cite web|url=https://www.kisantak.in/animal-husbandry/story/indias-first-clone-pt-bull-hisar-gaurav-in-cirb-increase-milking-and-re-production-nsr-1135812-2024-12-11|title=PT Bull: भैंसों का विक्की डोनर है ये बुल, रिसर्च में इस्तेमाल होता है सीमेन, पढ़ें इसकी खूबियां|website=Kisan Tak|date=11 December 2024}}</ref>.

==Selected publications==
* Yadav was first author of a 2024 Theriogenology study evaluating growth, health, telomere length and semen characteristics in cloned and re-cloned buffalo bulls<ref name="src-f264a3ee95">{{cite web|url=https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37793222/|title=Evaluation of postnatal growth, hematology, telomere length and semen attributes of multiple clones and re-clone of superior buffalo breeding bulls|website=PubMed|date=2024}}</ref>.
* Yadav co-authored a 2020 [[PLOS One|PLOS ONE]] study of the semen characteristics and fertility of a cloned water buffalo bull<ref name="src-a15f669816">{{cite web|url=https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0237766|title=Semen parameters and fertility potency of a cloned water buffalo bull produced from a semen-derived epithelial cell|website=PLOS ONE|date=21 August 2020}}</ref>.
* Yadav co-designed the research and was a corresponding author of the 2019 [[Scientific Reports]] paper “Successful cloning of a superior buffalo bull”<ref name="src-9c0f3d0c0c">{{cite web|url=https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-019-47909-8|title=Successful cloning of a superior buffalo bull|website=Scientific Reports|date=6 August 2019}}</ref>.
* Yadav co-authored a 2019 peer-reviewed review chapter on telomerase structure, regulation and measurement<ref name="src-8d2b29b28d">{{cite web|url=https://doi.org/10.5772/intechopen.89506|title=Telomerase Structure and Function, Activity and Its Regulation with Emerging Methods of Measurement in Eukaryotes|website=doi.org|date=24 December 2019}}</ref>.
* In 2018, Yadav co-authored the [[Current Science]] paper reporting the birth of Sach-Gaurav, the first cloned buffalo calf produced in the field at an Indian dairy farm<ref name="src-aa1862a70a">{{cite web|url=https://doi.org/10.18520/cs/v115/i2/198-198|title=Sach-Gaurav: World's First Cloned Buffalo Born In The Field At An Indian Dairy Farm|website=Current Science|date=2018}}</ref>.
* Yadav co-authored the book “Reproductive Biotechnology in Buffalo” (ISBN 8189304801)<ref name="src-11b51d3d09">{{cite web|url=https://ndri.res.in/sites/default/files/2025-06/ASAP%20April-2025.pdf|title=List of Indian e-Books of ASAP Publication (2018-20)|website=ICAR-National Dairy Research Institute|date=April 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251028060629/https://ndri.res.in/sites/default/files/2025-06/ASAP%20April-2025.pdf|archive-date=28 October 2025}}</ref>.
* Yadav was first author of the 2005 paper “Bovine ICM derived cells express the Oct4 ortholog” in Molecular Reproduction and Development<ref name="src-0c979bd135">{{cite web|url=https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15973686/|title=Bovine ICM derived cells express the Oct4 ortholog|website=PubMed|date=2005}}</ref>.

==Awards and recognition==
In December 2016, Yadav was elected a Fellow of the [[Society of Animal Physiologists of India]] (FSAPI)<ref name="src-c04a6d7ddb">{{cite web|url=https://sapi.in/list-of-life-members.html|title=Life Members and Fellows of SAPI|website=sapi.in|date=December 2016}}</ref>. Yadav led the Animal and Fisheries Sciences team that received the 2019 Nanaji Deshmukh [[Indian Council of Agricultural Research|ICAR]] Award for Outstanding Interdisciplinary Team Research; ICAR recorded 12 eligible applications for the two 2019 categories<ref name="src-4deaa5ee4f">{{cite web|url=https://icar.org.in/sites/default/files/2022-08/ICAR_Citations_final_2020_Low_0.pdf|title=ICAR Award 2019 Citations|website=Indian Council of Agricultural Research|date=2020}}</ref>. In 2019, the Indian Society for Buffalo Development conferred its Distinguished Scientist Award on Yadav at the society's national conference<ref name="src-d3e79cefed">{{cite web|url=https://cirb.res.in/news/national-conference-of-the-society/|title=Distinguished Scientist Award to Dr PS Yadav, ICAR-CIRB Hisar|website=ICAR-Central Institute for Research on Buffaloes|date=2019}}</ref>. The CIRB cloning team headed by Yadav received the Nanaji Deshmukh Team Award with a Rs 5 lakh prize for producing seven M-29 clones and a Hisar-Gaurav re-clone<ref name="src-83c8446026">{{cite web|url=https://www.bhaskar.com/local/haryana/hisar/news/cirb-hisars-name-entered-in-india-book-of-records-128550490.html|title=बड़ी उपलब्धि: सीआईआरबी हिसार का नाम इंडिया बुक ऑफ रिकॉर्ड में दर्ज; पहले मिल चुका नानाजी देशमुख टीम अवार्ड|website=Dainik Bhaskar|date=1 June 2021}}</ref>. Yadav delivered the Prof. S. S. Guraya Memorial Oration on technological advances in buffalo cloning at the Global Summit on Innovations in Reproductive Biology<ref name="src-4bb032ced9">{{cite web|url=https://www.bhu.ac.in/Images/files/Scientific%20Program%20Schedule%20GSIRB-ISSRF%20%202026.pdf|title=GSIRB-ISSRF 2026 Scientific Program Schedule|website=Banaras Hindu University, Varanasi|date=2026}}</ref>.

